<urn:atm:triage:finding/HTTPCLIENT-CONNECTERROR-MASKED-BY-TIMEOUT>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "HTTPCLIENT-CONNECTERROR-MASKED-BY-TIMEOUT" ;
  triage:title "Slow DNS/connect failure surfaces as generic request-budget-exceeded instead of the real connect error" ;
  triage:description "User ran `atm send arch-ctm@atm-dev.rand-mt.local 'ping'` with a hostname that does not resolve/connect, and got `HTTP client request exceeded its absolute request budget / Recovery: Retry after the bounded operation becomes available` -- a message that reads like transient overload, not host resolution failure. Root cause: HttpRuntimeClient::execute_with_deadline (crates/atm-http-runtime/src/client.rs:533-552) races self.connector.exchange(...) against tokio::time::timeout(remaining, ...). HttpRuntimeClientFailure already has a well-scoped Connect(String) variant (client.rs:50, populated at execute_reqwest_request's client.execute(...).map_err at line 486-488 with the real reqwest error, e.g. DNS/connect failure) that renders a clear message: 'HTTP client could not connect to the configured daemon endpoint' with the underlying cause attached (into_atm_error, line 61-64). But if DNS resolution or the TCP connect attempt for a bad host does not fail fast enough to beat the outer tokio::time::timeout race, the timeout branch wins, discards the still-in-flight connector future's result entirely (line 551: `.map_err(|_| HttpRuntimeClientFailure::Timeout.into_atm_error())?`, note the `_` throws away whatever the connector was doing), and returns the generic Timeout variant (line 74-77: 'HTTP client request exceeded its absolute request budget') instead. The hostname involved and the real underlying cause (unknown host / connection refused / etc.) are never surfaced to the user in this race outcome, even though the Connect variant exists specifically to carry that information. This is reproducible any time a configured cross-host destination's DNS lookup or TCP connect takes longer than the remaining request deadline -- which is the common case for a mistyped or unreachable hostname, since OS-level DNS resolution failure for a bogus name is frequently slow (multi-second resolver timeouts) rather than instant." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-al" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "error-message-quality" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-08-07T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AL10 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-08-07T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/HTTPCLIENT-CONNECTERROR-MASKED-BY-TIMEOUT/AL10-1> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "1" .

